  "title": "Muy --- The Same Word, Bitten Short",
  "drivablePrefix": 1,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e517207416be4826",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C60-muy",
      "sequence": 2365,
      "title": "muy — the same word, bitten short",
      "headword": "muy",
      "romanization": "muy",
      "gloss": "very — not a new word at all, but mucho with its ending bitten off, and the shape tells you what is coming next",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:4406da1bfffb8251",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have said mucho since chapter six — mucho gusto."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now you want to say that something is very something. Cansado is tired; how do you say very tired?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: muy",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Estoy muy cansado."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "muy is not a new word to learn. It is mucho, with its ending bitten off. Both are Latin multum, much — one kept whole, one worn short."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish does this a lot: a long form for standing alone, a short form for leaning on the word after it. You will meet the same trick again in this chapter's neighbours."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The two shapes are not interchangeable, and the rule is easy because the word tells you itself."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "mucho café, in front of a noun — the long form.",
                "muy grande, in front of an adjective — the short one.",
                "muy bien, in front of an adverb — the short one."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So muy never stands alone. If somebody asks how you are and you want to say very, you cannot answer muy — there is nothing for it to lean on. You would answer muy bien."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And this is the strongest very Spanish has in one word. Muy grande is as big as grande gets without reaching for another sentence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "muy bien, muy grande, muy cansado",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
